# PATCH: 003-restrict-clipboard
#
# Blocks all clipboard and drag-to-copy access, with an optional compile-time
# allow-list that permits in-browser copy/paste between specific sites.
#
# CORE BLOCKS (always active — six entry points):
#
# 1. Clipboard::ReadHelper (dom/events/Clipboard.cpp)
#    Blocks Clipboard.read() and Clipboard.readText() for all sites.
#
# 2. Clipboard::Write (dom/events/Clipboard.cpp)
#    Blocks Clipboard.write() / writeText() for all sites.
#
# 3. Document::ExecCommand (dom/base/Document.cpp)
#    Blocks document.execCommand("cut/copy/paste") for all sites.
#
# 4. nsCopySupport::FireClipboardEvent (dom/base/nsCopySupport.cpp)
#    Blocks the user-level copy command (Ctrl+C / right-click Copy) for ALL
#    content.  Paste (Ctrl+V) is intercepted here too for listed sites (see
#    INTERNAL CLIPBOARD below).
#
# 5. nsBaseClipboard::SetData (widget/nsBaseClipboard.cpp)
#    Backstop: suppresses every remaining OS clipboard write path.
#
# 6. nsBaseDragSession::InvokeDragSession (widget/nsBaseDragService.cpp)
#    Blocks all drag-and-drop to prevent text exfiltration via URL bar drag.
#
# INTERNAL CLIPBOARD (optional — activated by site-config.json):
#
#   When config/site-config.json lists one or more hostnames in
#   "clipboard_sites", an in-process clipboard is activated for those sites:
#
#   - Copy (Ctrl+C) on a listed site: FireClipboardEvent captures the selected
#     text via nsCopySupport::GetContents("text/plain", AllowCrossShadowBoundary
#     | SkipInvisibleContent), stores it in gDenInternalClipText, and then
#     empties the OS clipboard (nsIClipboard::EmptyClipboard IPC).  Emptying
#     the OS clipboard is the detection signal: if an external app later copies
#     something, the OS clipboard becomes non-empty again, and the paste
#     intercept falls back to serving OS content instead of internal text.
#
#   - Paste (Ctrl+V) on a listed site: FireClipboardEvent sets
#     gDenServePasteFromInternal = true in the paste return block (outside the
#     clipboardevents conditional — mozilla.cfg locks
#     dom.event.clipboardevents.enabled false, which skips the entire event-
#     dispatch block for web content, so the flag must be set after that block).
#     nsClipboardProxy::GetDataSnapshotSync (and GetData) first call
#     HasDataMatchingFlavors to check whether the OS clipboard has text/plain
#     content.  If it does, an external app copied after our wipe, so the
#     internal intercept is skipped and gDenInternalClipHasData is cleared (the
#     normal IPC path then serves the OS clipboard content).  If the OS
#     clipboard is empty, gDenInternalClipText is served directly without IPC.
#     The flag gDenServePasteFromInternal is NOT cleared by the intercept so
#     both a JS event.clipboardData.getData() call and the editor's default
#     paste (two separate clipboard reads in the same event) both succeed.
#
#   All globals (gDenInternalClipText, gDenInternalClipHasData,
#   gDenServePasteFromInternal) live in the content process.
#   nsClipboardProxy.cpp externs them from nsCopySupport.cpp — both compile
#   into the same content-process binary.  nsBaseClipboard.cpp (parent
#   process) is NOT involved in the paste path; its only DenBrowser role is
#   suppressing native OS clipboard writes in SetData.
#
#   Data copied from a listed site is never visible to the OS clipboard or to
#   any unlisted site.  External clipboard content (from other apps) can flow
#   IN to listed sites because the OS clipboard is non-empty in that case and
#   the intercept is bypassed.  build.sh injects the allow-list at compile time
#   using sentinel markers; empty config → full block, same as before.
#
# RELATIONSHIP TO PREFS:
#   lockPref("dom.allow_cut_copy", false) and
#   lockPref("dom.event.clipboardevents.enabled", false) in mozilla.cfg are
#   belt-and-suspenders for external sites.  This patch is the authoritative
#   clipboard control; the prefs do not affect listed-site internal clipboard.

